# More famous Welsh people, part G (12 November 2026): the gaps from the kings of Morgannwg to the scholars of the
# twentieth century, chosen with the Dictionary of Welsh Biography as a guide. Ifan: "I want as many historic people as
# possible on the site". Arthur's circle and the sixth century are covered by another thread, so are left out here.
# Same format and house voice as people2a.py (EDITORIAL.md: proud, nothing negative about Welsh figures, kings are kings,
# Britons not Celts, tradition in its own words).
from people2a import P

PEOPLE = [
 # ---------- The age of kings ----------
 P("morgan-hen", "kings", "d. 974", "Morgan Hen", "Morgan Hen", "King of Glywysing and Gwent", "Brenin Glywysing a Gwent", "Morgan_Hen_ab_Owain", "rulers",
   "The long-lived king who ruled the south-east of Wales for some forty years in the tenth century. Morgannwg, Morgan’s land, is held by many to be named after him.",
   "Y brenin hirhoedlog a reolodd dde-ddwyrain Cymru am ryw ddeugain mlynedd yn y ddegfed ganrif. Mae llawer yn credu mai ar ei ôl ef yr enwyd Morgannwg, gwlad Morgan.",
   ["Morgan ab Owain was king of Glywysing, the lands between the Tawe and the Usk, and for much of his reign of Gwent beyond it, so that he ruled the whole of the south-east from the Wye to the Tawe. He lived so long that the chronicles call him Morgan Hen, Morgan the Old, and when he died in 974 he had been a king for some forty years.",
    "He was a king of standing in the wider world of his day, and his name appears among the witnesses to the charters of the English kings Athelstan and Eadred. Welsh tradition holds that the kingdom of Morgannwg, Gwlad Morgan, took its name from him, though others give the honour to an earlier Morgan, son of Athrwys. Either way, the name of Glamorgan has carried a king’s name for more than a thousand years."],
   ["Morgan ab Owain oedd brenin Glywysing, y tiroedd rhwng Tawe ac Wysg, ac am ran helaeth o’i deyrnasiad brenin Gwent y tu hwnt iddi hefyd, fel ei fod yn rheoli’r de-ddwyrain cyfan o Wy i Dawe. Bu fyw mor hir nes i’r croniclau ei alw’n Morgan Hen, a phan fu farw yn 974 bu’n frenin am ryw ddeugain mlynedd.",
    "Roedd yn frenin o fri yn y byd ehangach, ac mae ei enw ymhlith tystion siarteri’r brenhinoedd Seisnig Athelstan ac Eadred. Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru cafodd teyrnas Morgannwg, Gwlad Morgan, ei henw ganddo ef, er bod eraill yn rhoi’r anrhydedd i Forgan cynharach, mab Athrwys. Y naill ffordd neu’r llall, mae enw Morgannwg wedi cario enw brenin am dros fil o flynyddoedd."]),
 P("iestyn-ap-gwrgant", "kings", "d. c. 1093", "Iestyn ap Gwrgant", "Iestyn ap Gwrgant", "The last king of Morgannwg", "Brenin olaf Morgannwg", "Iestyn_ap_Gwrgant", "rulers",
   "The last king of Morgannwg, who ruled the south-east of Wales until the Normans came in the 1090s. His sons and grandsons held the hills of Glamorgan for two hundred years more.",
   "Brenin olaf Morgannwg, a reolodd dde-ddwyrain Cymru nes i’r Normaniaid ddod yn y 1090au. Daliodd ei feibion a’i wyrion fryniau Morgannwg am ddau gan mlynedd arall.",
   ["Iestyn ap Gwrgant became king of Morgannwg after 1081, the last in a line of kings that went back to the days of Rome. In the early 1090s, after the fall of Rhys ap Tewdwr of Deheubarth, Robert Fitzhamon and his Norman knights pushed into the rich lowlands of the Vale and built their castle at Cardiff.",
    "Iestyn’s kingdom fell, but his family did not. His descendants kept the uplands of Glamorgan in Welsh hands, and the lords of Afan, of his line, ruled from their hill country around Aberafan and Margam for some two hundred years more. Many families of Glamorgan proudly traced their pedigrees to the last king of Morgannwg, and the Welsh tradition of the county has kept his name ever since."],
   ["Daeth Iestyn ap Gwrgant yn frenin Morgannwg wedi 1081, yr olaf mewn llinach o frenhinoedd a âi’n ôl i oes Rhufain. Yn gynnar yn y 1090au, wedi cwymp Rhys ap Tewdwr o Ddeheubarth, gwthiodd Robert Fitzhamon a’i farchogion Normanaidd i wastadeddau cyfoethog y Fro a chodi eu castell yng Nghaerdydd.",
    "Syrthiodd teyrnas Iestyn, ond nid ei deulu. Cadwodd ei ddisgynyddion ucheldir Morgannwg yn nwylo’r Cymry, a theyrnasodd arglwyddi Afan, o’i linach ef, o’u bröydd mynyddig o gwmpas Aberafan a Margam am ryw ddau gan mlynedd arall. Olrheiniodd llawer o deuluoedd Morgannwg eu hachau’n falch at frenin olaf Morgannwg, ac mae traddodiad Cymraeg y sir wedi cadw ei enw byth oddi ar hynny."]),

 # ---------- The age of the princes ----------
 P("madog-ap-maredudd", "medieval", "d. 1160", "Madog ap Maredudd", "Madog ap Maredudd", "The last king of all Powys", "Brenin olaf Powys gyfan", "Madog_ap_Maredudd", "rulers",
   "King of Powys from 1132 to 1160, who kept his kingdom strong between Gwynedd and England and gathered the great poets to his court. He was the last to rule the whole of Powys.",
   "Brenin Powys o 1132 hyd 1160, a gadwodd ei deyrnas yn gryf rhwng Gwynedd a Lloegr a chasglu’r beirdd mawr i’w lys. Ef oedd yr olaf i reoli Powys gyfan.",
   ["Madog ap Maredudd ruled Powys for almost thirty years, from the Berwyn to the borders of England. Pressed between the power of Owain Gwynedd to the west and the English crown to the east, he held his land together by skill and by the sword, and pushed his rule east as far as Oswestry. His daughter Gwenllian married the Lord Rhys of Deheubarth.",
    "His court was a home of poetry. Gwalchmai ap Meilyr and Cynddelw Brydydd Mawr sang his praise, and when he died at Winchester in 1160 and was brought home to be buried at Meifod, Cynddelw wrote his elegy. After him Powys was divided for ever, into Powys Wenwynwyn in the south and Powys Fadog in the north, named after his grandson Madog. The Welsh poets remembered his reign as the golden age of Powys."],
   ["Teyrnasodd Madog ap Maredudd ar Bowys am bron i ddeng mlynedd ar hugain, o’r Berwyn hyd ororau Lloegr. Wedi ei wasgu rhwng grym Owain Gwynedd i’r gorllewin a choron Lloegr i’r dwyrain, cadwodd ei wlad ynghyd drwy fedr a thrwy’r cleddyf, a gwthio’i reolaeth tua’r dwyrain hyd Groesoswallt. Priododd ei ferch Gwenllian â’r Arglwydd Rhys o Ddeheubarth.",
    "Roedd ei lys yn gartref i farddoniaeth. Canodd Gwalchmai ap Meilyr a Chynddelw Brydydd Mawr ei glod, a phan fu farw yng Nghaerwynt yn 1160 a’i ddwyn adref i’w gladdu ym Meifod, Cynddelw a ganodd ei farwnad. Ar ei ôl rhannwyd Powys am byth, yn Bowys Wenwynwyn yn y de a Phowys Fadog yn y gogledd, a enwyd ar ôl ei ŵyr Madog. Cofiai’r beirdd ei deyrnasiad fel oes aur Powys."]),
 P("gruffudd-ap-rhys", "medieval", "c. 1090–1137", "Gruffudd ap Rhys", "Gruffudd ap Rhys", "Prince of Deheubarth, husband of Gwenllian", "Tywysog Deheubarth, gŵr Gwenllian", "Gruffydd_ap_Rhys", "rulers",
   "Son of King Rhys ap Tewdwr, who grew up in exile in Ireland and came home to win back Deheubarth from the Normans. He was the husband of Gwenllian and the father of the Lord Rhys.",
   "Mab y Brenin Rhys ap Tewdwr, a fagwyd yn alltud yn Iwerddon ac a ddaeth adref i adennill Deheubarth oddi ar y Normaniaid. Ef oedd gŵr Gwenllian a thad yr Arglwydd Rhys.",
   ["Gruffudd was a small boy when his father, Rhys ap Tewdwr, the last king of Deheubarth, was killed in 1093 and the Normans swept over the south. He was taken to safety in Ireland. In about 1113 he came home, and in 1116 he rose, attacking the Norman castles at Narberth, Llandovery and Swansea, and the young men of Deheubarth flocked to him as the heir of their kings.",
    "He married Gwenllian, daughter of Gruffudd ap Cynan of Gwynedd, and together they led the long fight from the forests of Cantref Mawr. In 1136, after Gwenllian fell at Cydweli, Gruffudd joined her brothers Owain and Cadwaladr and won the great victory of Crug Mawr near Cardigan. He died the next year. The chronicle of the princes mourned him as the light and strength of the men of the south, and his son, the Lord Rhys, made Deheubarth great again."],
   ["Bachgen bach oedd Gruffudd pan laddwyd ei dad, Rhys ap Tewdwr, brenin olaf Deheubarth, yn 1093, ac ysgubodd y Normaniaid dros y de. Aed ag ef i ddiogelwch yn Iwerddon. Tua 1113 daeth adref, ac yn 1116 cododd mewn gwrthryfel, gan ymosod ar gestyll Normanaidd Arberth, Llanymddyfri ac Abertawe, a thyrrodd gwŷr ifanc Deheubarth ato fel etifedd eu brenhinoedd.",
    "Priododd Wenllian, merch Gruffudd ap Cynan o Wynedd, a gyda’i gilydd arweiniasant y frwydr hir o goedwigoedd y Cantref Mawr. Yn 1136, wedi i Wenllian syrthio yng Nghydweli, ymunodd Gruffudd â’i brodyr Owain a Chadwaladr ac ennill buddugoliaeth fawr Crug Mawr ger Aberteifi. Bu farw’r flwyddyn ganlynol. Galarodd cronicl y tywysogion amdano fel goleuni a nerth gwŷr y de, ac fe wnaeth ei fab, yr Arglwydd Rhys, Ddeheubarth yn fawr unwaith eto."]),
 P("prydydd-y-moch", "medieval", "fl. 1173–1220", "Prydydd y Moch", "Prydydd y Moch", "Chief poet of Llywelyn Fawr", "Pencerdd Llywelyn Fawr", "Llywarch_ap_Llywelyn", "words",
   "Llywarch ap Llywelyn, called Prydydd y Moch, the greatest poet of Gwynedd in the age of Llywelyn Fawr, who sang more poems in praise of the prince than anyone else.",
   "Llywarch ap Llywelyn, a elwid Prydydd y Moch, bardd mwyaf Gwynedd yn oes Llywelyn Fawr, a ganodd fwy o gerddi i’r tywysog nag unrhyw un arall.",
   ["Llywarch ap Llywelyn sang to the princes of Gwynedd for nearly fifty years, from the sons of Owain Gwynedd to Llywelyn ap Iorwerth, Llywelyn Fawr, whom he followed from the start of his rise. Nobody knows for certain why he was called Prydydd y Moch, the Poet of the Pigs. His great poems ring with the clash of battle and the glory of the prince who was making Gwynedd the leader of Wales.",
    "In one famous poem he offered to carry red-hot iron in his hand before the prince, to prove that he was innocent of a killing he was charged with. Most of his work was kept in the Hendregadredd Manuscript, the great book of the Poets of the Princes, now in the National Library of Wales. For the princes of Gwynedd he was what Taliesin had been for Urien: the voice that made their deeds live."],
   ["Canodd Llywarch ap Llywelyn i dywysogion Gwynedd am bron i hanner can mlynedd, o feibion Owain Gwynedd hyd Lywelyn ab Iorwerth, Llywelyn Fawr, a ddilynodd o ddechrau ei esgyniad. Ni ŵyr neb i sicrwydd pam y’i gelwid yn Brydydd y Moch. Mae ei gerddi mawr yn atseinio â thwrf brwydr a gogoniant y tywysog a oedd yn gwneud Gwynedd yn arweinydd Cymru.",
    "Mewn un gerdd enwog cynigiodd gario haearn poeth yn ei law gerbron y tywysog, i brofi ei fod yn ddieuog o laddiad y’i cyhuddwyd ohono. Cadwyd y rhan fwyaf o’i waith yn Llawysgrif Hendregadredd, llyfr mawr Beirdd y Tywysogion, sydd bellach yn y Llyfrgell Genedlaethol. I dywysogion Gwynedd ef oedd yr hyn a fu Taliesin i Urien: y llais a gadwodd eu gweithredoedd yn fyw."]),
 P("gruffudd-ap-llywelyn-fawr", "medieval", "c. 1200–1244", "Gruffudd ap Llywelyn Fawr", "Gruffudd ap Llywelyn Fawr", "Son of Llywelyn Fawr, father of the last prince", "Mab Llywelyn Fawr, tad y tywysog olaf", "Gruffydd_ap_Llywelyn_Fawr", "rulers",
   "The eldest son of Llywelyn Fawr and the father of Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, the last prince. He died in 1244 trying to escape from the Tower of London.",
   "Mab hynaf Llywelyn Fawr a thad Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, y tywysog olaf. Bu farw yn 1244 wrth geisio dianc o Dŵr Llundain.",
   ["Gruffudd was the eldest son of Llywelyn Fawr, born to Tangwystl before his father married Joan. As a boy he was sent to King John as a hostage, and he spent years in English hands. He grew up to be a bold soldier, loved by many in Gwynedd, who saw in him the heir of the old Welsh law, under which every son of a prince had his share.",
    "After his father’s death in 1240 he was given up to King Henry III, and held in the Tower of London. On the night of 1 March 1244, Saint David’s Day, he knotted his sheets and hangings into a rope and climbed out of a high window. The rope broke, and he fell to his death. His death set Wales alight: his brother Dafydd rose against Henry, and Gruffudd’s own son Llywelyn would become Prince of Wales, the only Welsh prince the English crown ever recognised by that title."],
   ["Gruffudd oedd mab hynaf Llywelyn Fawr, a anwyd i Dangwystl cyn i’w dad briodi Siwan. Yn fachgen fe’i hanfonwyd at y Brenin John yn wystl, a threuliodd flynyddoedd yn nwylo’r Saeson. Tyfodd yn filwr eofn, a gerid gan lawer yng Ngwynedd, a welai ynddo etifedd yr hen gyfraith Gymreig, lle’r oedd gan bob mab i dywysog ei gyfran.",
    "Wedi marw ei dad yn 1240 fe’i rhoddwyd i’r Brenin Harri III, a’i garcharu yn Nhŵr Llundain. Ar noson 1 Mawrth 1244, Dydd Gŵyl Dewi, clymodd ei gynfasau a’i lenni’n rhaff a dringo allan o ffenestr uchel. Torrodd y rhaff, a syrthiodd i’w farwolaeth. Taniodd ei farwolaeth Gymru: cododd ei frawd Dafydd yn erbyn Harri, a daeth mab Gruffudd ei hun, Llywelyn, yn Dywysog Cymru, yr unig dywysog Cymreig a gydnabu coron Lloegr erioed wrth y teitl hwnnw."]),
 P("eleanor-de-montfort", "medieval", "1252–1282", "Eleanor de Montfort", "Elinor de Montfort", "Princess of Wales, wife of Llywelyn", "Tywysoges Cymru, gwraig Llywelyn", "Eleanor_de_Montfort", "rulers",
   "The wife of Llywelyn ap Gruffudd and the first woman to be called Princess of Wales. Captured at sea on her way to marry him, she waited three years to become his bride.",
   "Gwraig Llywelyn ap Gruffudd a’r fenyw gyntaf i’w galw’n Dywysoges Cymru. Fe’i daliwyd ar y môr ar ei ffordd i’w briodi, ac arhosodd dair blynedd i ddod yn wraig iddo.",
   ["Eleanor was the daughter of Simon de Montfort, the great English rebel, and granddaughter of King John. Her father and Llywelyn ap Gruffudd had been allies, and she was promised to the prince. In 1275 she was married to him by proxy in France and set sail for Wales, but off the Isles of Scilly her ship was seized by men of Edward I, who held her prisoner for three years.",
    "Llywelyn did not give her up. At last, in October 1278, they were married in Worcester Cathedral. In Wales she was Princess of Wales and Lady of Snowdon, and the letters she wrote in her own name are the first by any woman to bear that title. On 19 June 1282, at the court of Garth Celyn by Abergwyngregyn, she died giving birth to their daughter, Gwenllian. She was buried in the friary at Llan-faes in Môn, six months before her husband fell at Cilmeri."],
   ["Merch Simon de Montfort, y gwrthryfelwr Seisnig mawr, oedd Elinor, ac wyres i’r Brenin John. Bu ei thad a Llywelyn ap Gruffudd yn gynghreiriaid, ac addawyd hi i’r tywysog. Yn 1275 fe’i priodwyd ag ef drwy ddirprwy yn Ffrainc a hwyliodd am Gymru, ond ger Ynysoedd Sili cipiwyd ei llong gan wŷr Edward I, a’i daliodd yn garcharor am dair blynedd.",
    "Ni roddodd Llywelyn hi heibio. O’r diwedd, ym mis Hydref 1278, priodwyd hwy yn Eglwys Gadeiriol Caerwrangon. Yng Nghymru hi oedd Tywysoges Cymru ac Arglwyddes Eryri, a’r llythyrau a ysgrifennodd yn ei henw ei hun yw’r cyntaf gan unrhyw fenyw yn dwyn y teitl hwnnw. Ar 19 Mehefin 1282, yn llys Garth Celyn ger Abergwyngregyn, bu farw wrth eni eu merch, Gwenllian. Fe’i claddwyd ym mrodordy Llan-faes ym Môn, chwe mis cyn i’w gŵr syrthio yng Nghilmeri."],
   first=("wales", "The first woman to be called Princess of Wales, 1278", "Y fenyw gyntaf i’w galw’n Dywysoges Cymru, 1278", 1278)),
 P("gruffudd-ab-yr-ynad-coch", "medieval", "fl. 1277–1283", "Gruffudd ab yr Ynad Coch", "Gruffudd ab yr Ynad Coch", "Poet of the great elegy for Llywelyn", "Bardd y farwnad fawr i Lywelyn", "Gruffudd_ab_yr_Ynad_Coch", "words",
   "The poet who wrote the elegy for Llywelyn ap Gruffudd after his death in 1282, the greatest lament in the Welsh language.",
   "Y bardd a ganodd y farwnad i Lywelyn ap Gruffudd wedi ei farw yn 1282, y galargan fwyaf yn yr iaith Gymraeg.",
   ["Gruffudd was the son of a judge, yr Ynad Coch, the Red Justice, from a family of lawyers who served the princes of Gwynedd. Only a few of his poems survive, but one of them is counted by many as the greatest poem in Welsh: his elegy for Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, the last prince, killed at Cilmeri on 11 December 1282.",
    "In it the whole world shakes at the fall of the prince. ‘Poni welwch-chwi hynt y gwynt a’r glaw? Poni welwch-chwi’r deri’n ymdaraw?’, he asks: do you not see the rushing of the wind and the rain, do you not see the oak trees beating together? The sea floods the land, the sun hurtles across the sky, the stars have fallen, and he asks at last whether this is the end of the world. Seven hundred years later the poem is still learnt in Welsh schools, and it still carries the grief of a nation."],
   ["Mab i ynad, yr Ynad Coch, oedd Gruffudd, o deulu o gyfreithwyr a wasanaethai dywysogion Gwynedd. Ychydig o’i gerddi sydd wedi goroesi, ond mae llawer yn cyfrif un ohonynt yn gerdd fwyaf y Gymraeg: ei farwnad i Lywelyn ap Gruffudd, y tywysog olaf, a laddwyd yng Nghilmeri ar 11 Rhagfyr 1282.",
    "Ynddi mae’r byd i gyd yn crynu wrth gwymp y tywysog. ‘Poni welwch-chwi hynt y gwynt a’r glaw? Poni welwch-chwi’r deri’n ymdaraw?’ gofynna. Mae’r môr yn llifo dros y tir, yr haul yn rhuthro ar draws yr wybr, y sêr wedi syrthio, ac mae’n gofyn yn y diwedd onid diwedd y byd yw hyn. Saith gan mlynedd yn ddiweddarach mae’r gerdd yn cael ei dysgu yn ysgolion Cymru o hyd, ac mae’n dal i gario galar cenedl."]),
 P("llywelyn-ap-gruffudd-fychan", "medieval", "d. 1401", "Llywelyn ap Gruffudd Fychan", "Llywelyn ap Gruffudd Fychan", "The hero of Llandovery", "Arwr Llanymddyfri", "Llywelyn_ap_Gruffudd_Fychan", "heroes",
   "A gentleman of Caeo who led the army of Henry IV astray to protect Owain Glyndŵr, and was put to death for it at Llandovery in 1401. His statue stands there today.",
   "Uchelwr o Gaeo a arweiniodd fyddin Harri IV ar gyfeiliorn i amddiffyn Owain Glyndŵr, ac a ddienyddiwyd am hynny yn Llanymddyfri yn 1401. Saif ei gerflun yno heddiw.",
   ["Llywelyn ap Gruffudd Fychan was a wealthy gentleman of Caeo in Carmarthenshire, famous for his open house. Adam of Usk wrote that sixteen tuns of wine were drunk at his table every year. In October 1401 Henry IV came to Llandovery with his army to hunt down Owain Glyndŵr, and Llywelyn offered to lead the king to the rebels.",
    "Two of his own sons were with Owain. For days he led the royal army up and down the hills of Deheubarth, far from where Glyndŵr’s men were. When the king understood what he had done, Llywelyn was put to death in the square at Llandovery on 9 October 1401, before the king’s eyes, and he did not say a word to save himself. In 2001, six hundred years later, a great steel statue of him was raised beside Llandovery castle, looking out over the place where he died for Owain."],
   ["Uchelwr cyfoethog o Gaeo yn Sir Gaerfyrddin oedd Llywelyn ap Gruffudd Fychan, ac enwog am ei groeso. Ysgrifennodd Adda o Frynbuga fod un tunnell ar bymtheg o win yn cael eu hyfed wrth ei fwrdd bob blwyddyn. Ym mis Hydref 1401 daeth Harri IV i Lanymddyfri gyda’i fyddin i hela Owain Glyndŵr, a chynigiodd Llywelyn arwain y brenin at y gwrthryfelwyr.",
    "Roedd dau o’i feibion ei hun gydag Owain. Am ddyddiau arweiniodd y fyddin frenhinol i fyny ac i lawr bryniau Deheubarth, ymhell o’r fan lle’r oedd gwŷr Glyndŵr. Pan ddeallodd y brenin yr hyn a wnaethai, dienyddiwyd Llywelyn ar y sgwâr yn Llanymddyfri ar 9 Hydref 1401, o flaen llygaid y brenin, ac ni ddywedodd air i’w achub ei hun. Yn 2001, chwe chan mlynedd yn ddiweddarach, codwyd cerflun dur mawr ohono wrth gastell Llanymddyfri, yn edrych dros y man lle bu farw dros Owain."]),
 P("william-herbert", "medieval", "c. 1423–1469", "William Herbert, Earl of Pembroke", "Wiliam Herbert, Iarll Penfro", "Lord of Raglan, the first Welsh earl", "Arglwydd Rhaglan, yr iarll Cymreig cyntaf", "William_Herbert,_1st_Earl_of_Pembroke_(died_1469)", "rulers",
   "The lord of Raglan who rose to be the most powerful man in Wales, the first Welshman to sit among the lords of England, and the guardian of the young Henry Tudor.",
   "Arglwydd Rhaglan a ddaeth yn ŵr mwyaf grymus Cymru, y Cymro cyntaf i eistedd ymhlith arglwyddi Lloegr, a gwarcheidwad y Harri Tudur ifanc.",
   ["William Herbert was the son of Sir William ap Thomas, who built the great tower at Raglan, and Gwladys, daughter of Dafydd Gam. In the Wars of the Roses he stood with the house of York, and Edward IV made him a lord in 1461, the first Welshman to sit in the House of Lords. He finished Raglan Castle as one of the grandest houses in Britain, and he governed much of Wales for the king. In 1468 he was made Earl of Pembroke.",
    "He was a patron of the Welsh poets, and they saw in him a leader for the whole nation. When he took Harlech in 1468, Guto’r Glyn urged him to unite Wales: ‘Dwg Forgannwg a Gwynedd, gwna’n un o Gonwy i Nedd’, bring Glamorgan and Gwynedd together, make it one from Conwy to Neath. The young Henry Tudor grew up in his household at Raglan. William Herbert was killed after the battle of Edgecote in 1469, and the poets mourned him as the pillar of Wales."],
   ["Mab Syr Wiliam ap Tomos, a gododd y tŵr mawr yn Rhaglan, a Gwladys, merch Dafydd Gam, oedd Wiliam Herbert. Yn Rhyfeloedd y Rhosynnau safodd gyda thŷ Iorc, a gwnaeth Edward IV ef yn arglwydd yn 1461, y Cymro cyntaf i eistedd yn Nhŷ’r Arglwyddi. Gorffennodd Gastell Rhaglan yn un o dai mwyaf ysblennydd Prydain, a llywodraethodd ran helaeth o Gymru dros y brenin. Yn 1468 fe’i gwnaed yn Iarll Penfro.",
    "Roedd yn noddwr i feirdd Cymru, a gwelent ynddo arweinydd i’r genedl gyfan. Pan gipiodd Harlech yn 1468, anogodd Guto’r Glyn ef i uno Cymru: ‘Dwg Forgannwg a Gwynedd, gwna’n un o Gonwy i Nedd.’ Magwyd Harri Tudur ifanc ar ei aelwyd yn Rhaglan. Lladdwyd Wiliam Herbert wedi brwydr Edgecote yn 1469, a galarodd y beirdd amdano fel colofn Cymru."],
   first=("wales", "The first Welshman to sit in the House of Lords, 1461", "Y Cymro cyntaf i eistedd yn Nhŷ’r Arglwyddi, 1461", 1461)),

 # ---------- The Tudors and Stuarts ----------
 P("humphrey-llwyd", "tudor", "1527–1568", "Humphrey Llwyd", "Humphrey Llwyd", "Maker of the first map of Wales", "Lluniwr y map cyntaf o Gymru", "Humphrey_Llwyd", "learning",
   "The scholar of Denbigh who drew the first printed map of Wales, wrote its history, and helped pass the Act of 1563 that gave Wales the Bible in Welsh.",
   "Ysgolhaig o Ddinbych a luniodd y map printiedig cyntaf o Gymru, ysgrifennu ei hanes, a helpu i basio Deddf 1563 a roddodd y Beibl yn Gymraeg i Gymru.",
   ["Humphrey Llwyd was born in Denbigh, studied at Oxford, and became physician to the Earl of Arundel. He was a man of every kind of learning: doctor, collector of books, historian and mapmaker. His Cronica Walliae of 1559, the history of the Welsh princes, was the book that David Powel printed in 1584 as The Historie of Cambria, the first history of Wales in print.",
    "As member of Parliament for Denbigh he helped to steer through the Act of 1563 that ordered the Bible and the Prayer Book to be translated into Welsh. Before he died in 1568 he sent his map of Wales to his friend Abraham Ortelius in Antwerp, who printed it in 1573 in the great atlas of the world. Cambriae Typus was the first printed map of Wales as a country of its own, and for a century it was the map of Wales that Europe knew."],
   ["Ganed Humphrey Llwyd yn Ninbych, astudiodd yn Rhydychen, a daeth yn feddyg i Iarll Arundel. Roedd yn ŵr o bob math o ddysg: meddyg, casglwr llyfrau, hanesydd a gwneuthurwr mapiau. Ei Cronica Walliae yn 1559, hanes tywysogion Cymru, oedd y llyfr a argraffodd David Powel yn 1584 fel The Historie of Cambria, yr hanes cyntaf o Gymru mewn print.",
    "Fel aelod seneddol dros Ddinbych helpodd i lywio drwy’r Senedd Ddeddf 1563 a orchmynnodd gyfieithu’r Beibl a’r Llyfr Gweddi i’r Gymraeg. Cyn iddo farw yn 1568 anfonodd ei fap o Gymru at ei gyfaill Abraham Ortelius yn Antwerp, a’i hargraffodd yn 1573 yn atlas mawr y byd. Cambriae Typus oedd y map printiedig cyntaf o Gymru fel gwlad ar ei phen ei hun, ac am ganrif hwnnw oedd y map o Gymru a adwaenai Ewrop."],
   first=("wales", "The first printed map of Wales as a country of its own, 1573", "Y map printiedig cyntaf o Gymru fel gwlad ar ei phen ei hun, 1573", 1573)),
 P("richard-gwyn", "tudor", "c. 1537–1584", "Saint Richard Gwyn", "Sant Richard Gwyn", "Schoolmaster, poet and martyr", "Ysgolfeistr, bardd a merthyr", "Richard_Gwyn", "faith",
   "A schoolmaster from Llanidloes who would not give up his Catholic faith. He wrote Welsh carols in prison, died for his faith at Wrexham in 1584, and was made a saint in 1970.",
   "Ysgolfeistr o Lanidloes na fynnai ildio’i ffydd Gatholig. Ysgrifennodd garolau Cymraeg yn y carchar, bu farw dros ei ffydd yn Wrecsam yn 1584, a’i wneud yn sant yn 1970.",
   ["Richard Gwyn was born at Llanidloes and studied at Cambridge, then came home to teach school in the Wrexham country. When the laws against Catholics grew harsh under Elizabeth I, he held to the old faith of his fathers. He was arrested again and again, fined and held in chains, and for four years he was kept in prison.",
    "In prison he wrote poems in Welsh, carols in the old free metres, calling the people of Wales to stay true. He was put to death at Wrexham on 15 October 1584, the first Welsh martyr of Elizabeth’s reign, and he met his death with courage. In 1970 Pope Paul VI made him a saint, one of the Forty Martyrs of England and Wales, and schools across Wales carry his name."],
   ["Ganed Richard Gwyn yn Llanidloes ac astudiodd yng Nghaergrawnt, yna daeth adref i gadw ysgol yng nghyffiniau Wrecsam. Pan aeth y deddfau yn erbyn Catholigion yn llym dan Elisabeth I, glynodd wrth hen ffydd ei dadau. Fe’i daliwyd dro ar ôl tro, ei ddirwyo a’i gadw mewn cadwyni, ac am bedair blynedd fe’i cadwyd yn y carchar.",
    "Yn y carchar ysgrifennodd gerddi yn Gymraeg, carolau yn yr hen fesurau rhydd, yn galw ar bobl Cymru i aros yn ffyddlon. Fe’i dienyddiwyd yn Wrecsam ar 15 Hydref 1584, y merthyr Cymreig cyntaf yn oes Elisabeth, a wynebodd ei farwolaeth yn ddewr. Yn 1970 gwnaeth y Pab Pawl VI ef yn sant, un o Ddeugain Merthyr Cymru a Lloegr, ac mae ysgolion ledled Cymru’n dwyn ei enw."]),
 P("john-penry", "tudor", "1563–1593", "John Penry", "John Penri", "Who pleaded for preaching in Welsh", "A ymbiliodd am bregethu yn Gymraeg", "John_Penry", "faith",
   "A young Puritan from Breconshire who pleaded with Queen Elizabeth and Parliament for the Gospel to be preached to the people of Wales in their own language, and died for his faith at thirty.",
   "Piwritan ifanc o Frycheiniog a ymbiliodd ar y Frenhines Elisabeth a’r Senedd i bregethu’r Efengyl i bobl Cymru yn eu hiaith eu hunain, ac a fu farw dros ei ffydd yn ddeg ar hugain oed.",
   ["John Penry was born in 1563 at Cefn Brith, on the slopes of Mynydd Epynt near Llangamarch, and studied at Cambridge and Oxford. He grieved that the people of Wales had so few preachers, and none who would speak to them in Welsh. In 1587 he published A Treatise containing the Aequity of an Humble Supplication, asking the Queen and Parliament to send preachers who could teach the people of Wales in their own tongue.",
    "His bold writings brought the anger of the bishops, and he had to flee to Scotland. When he came back to London he was arrested, and on 29 May 1593 he was put to death, at the age of thirty, leaving a wife and four small daughters. The Welsh nonconformists honoured him as their first martyr, and the Welsh press that published Gwynfor Evans’s Land of My Fathers was named Gwasg John Penry after him."],
   ["Ganed John Penri yn 1563 yng Nghefn Brith, ar lethrau Mynydd Epynt ger Llangamarch, ac astudiodd yng Nghaergrawnt a Rhydychen. Galarai fod gan bobl Cymru gyn lleied o bregethwyr, a neb a siaradai â hwy yn Gymraeg. Yn 1587 cyhoeddodd A Treatise containing the Aequity of an Humble Supplication, yn gofyn i’r Frenhines a’r Senedd anfon pregethwyr a allai ddysgu pobl Cymru yn eu hiaith eu hunain.",
    "Daeth ei ysgrifau eofn â llid yr esgobion arno, a bu’n rhaid iddo ffoi i’r Alban. Pan ddaeth yn ôl i Lundain fe’i daliwyd, ac ar 29 Mai 1593 fe’i dienyddiwyd, yn ddeg ar hugain oed, gan adael gwraig a phedair merch fach. Anrhydeddodd yr anghydffurfwyr Cymreig ef fel eu merthyr cyntaf, ac enwyd y wasg Gymraeg a gyhoeddodd Aros Mae Gwynfor Evans yn Wasg John Penri ar ei ôl."]),
 P("william-wroth", "tudor", "1576–1641", "William Wroth", "William Wroth", "Founder of the first chapel in Wales", "Sylfaenydd y capel cyntaf yng Nghymru", "William_Wroth", "faith",
   "The rector of Llanfaches in Gwent, a preacher so loved that crowds came from far and wide to hear him, who in 1639 founded the first nonconformist church in Wales.",
   "Rheithor Llanfaches yng Ngwent, pregethwr mor annwyl nes i dyrfaoedd ddod o bell ac agos i’w glywed, a sefydlodd yn 1639 yr eglwys anghydffurfiol gyntaf yng Nghymru.",
   ["William Wroth became rector of Llanfaches, between Newport and Chepstow, in 1617. He was a plain, warm preacher, and people walked across Gwent and over the Severn from Bristol to hear him. Some said he was the first in his age to preach in the open air in Wales.",
    "In November 1639, with the help of Henry Jessey of London, he gathered the godly of the district into a church of their own at Llanfaches, a gathered church after the pattern of the New England churches. It was the first nonconformist church in Wales, and from it the chapels of Wales were born: the Independents, the Baptists, and in time the thousands of chapels that shaped the life of the nation. Llanfaches was called the Antioch of Wales."],
   ["Daeth William Wroth yn rheithor Llanfaches, rhwng Casnewydd a Chas-gwent, yn 1617. Pregethwr plaen a chynnes ydoedd, a cherddai pobl ar draws Gwent a thros Hafren o Fryste i’w glywed. Dywedai rhai mai ef oedd y cyntaf yn ei oes i bregethu yn yr awyr agored yng Nghymru.",
    "Ym mis Tachwedd 1639, gyda chymorth Henry Jessey o Lundain, casglodd bobl dduwiol yr ardal yn eglwys eu hunain yn Llanfaches, eglwys gynulledig ar batrwm eglwysi Lloegr Newydd. Hon oedd yr eglwys anghydffurfiol gyntaf yng Nghymru, ac ohoni y ganed capeli Cymru: yr Annibynwyr, y Bedyddwyr, ac ymhen amser y miloedd o gapeli a luniodd fywyd y genedl. Galwyd Llanfaches yn Antiochia Cymru."],
   first=("wales", "Founded the first nonconformist church in Wales, Llanfaches 1639", "Sefydlodd yr eglwys anghydffurfiol gyntaf yng Nghymru, Llanfaches 1639", 1639)),
 P("daniel-rowland", "tudor", "1713–1790", "Daniel Rowland", "Daniel Rowland", "The great preacher of Llangeitho", "Pregethwr mawr Llangeitho", "Daniel_Rowland", "faith",
   "With Howell Harris the leader of the Methodist revival in Wales. Thousands walked across Wales to hear him preach at Llangeitho in Ceredigion.",
   "Gyda Howel Harris, arweinydd y diwygiad Methodistaidd yng Nghymru. Cerddai miloedd ar draws Cymru i’w glywed yn pregethu yn Llangeitho yng Ngheredigion.",
   ["Daniel Rowland was the curate of Llangeitho, a quiet parish in the hills of Ceredigion, when in about 1735 he heard Griffith Jones of Llanddowror preach and his life was changed. He began to preach with a fire that nobody in Wales had heard before, and in the same year as Howell Harris he set the Methodist revival alight.",
    "For more than fifty years people came to Llangeitho from every corner of Wales. On the Sundays of the communion, thousands walked over the mountains from Gwynedd and Glamorgan to hear him, singing as they went. William Williams Pantycelyn was his friend and fellow worker. A statue of Daniel Rowland stands in the village today, and the movement he led became the Calvinistic Methodist church, the Presbyterian Church of Wales."],
   ["Curad Llangeitho, plwyf tawel ym mryniau Ceredigion, oedd Daniel Rowland pan glywodd, tua 1735, Griffith Jones o Landdowror yn pregethu, a newidiwyd ei fywyd. Dechreuodd bregethu â thân na chlywsai neb yng Nghymru o’r blaen, ac yn yr un flwyddyn â Howel Harris taniodd y diwygiad Methodistaidd.",
    "Am dros hanner can mlynedd daeth pobl i Langeitho o bob cwr o Gymru. Ar Suliau’r cymun cerddai miloedd dros y mynyddoedd o Wynedd a Morgannwg i’w glywed, gan ganu ar y ffordd. William Williams Pantycelyn oedd ei gyfaill a’i gydweithiwr. Saif cerflun o Daniel Rowland yn y pentref heddiw, a daeth y mudiad a arweiniodd yn Eglwys y Methodistiaid Calfinaidd, Eglwys Bresbyteraidd Cymru."]),

 # ---------- Industry and empire ----------
 P("thomas-williams-llanidan", "industrial", "1737–1802", "Thomas Williams", "Thomas Williams, Llanidan", "The Copper King of Parys Mountain", "Brenin Copr Mynydd Parys", "Thomas_Williams_of_Llanidan", "enterprise",
   "An Anglesey lawyer who became the Copper King, master of Parys Mountain, the greatest copper mine in the world, whose copper sheathed the ships of the Royal Navy.",
   "Cyfreithiwr o Fôn a ddaeth yn Frenin Copr, meistr Mynydd Parys, y gwaith copr mwyaf yn y byd, a’i gopr yn gorchuddio llongau’r Llynges.",
   ["In 1768 a great body of copper ore was found on Parys Mountain near Amlwch. Thomas Williams of Llanidan, a country lawyer, took up the cause of the landowners and by the 1780s was master of the mines. Parys Mountain became the greatest copper mine in the world, and Amlwch grew from a hamlet into one of the largest towns in Wales.",
    "Williams built smelting works at Holywell, at Swansea and in Lancashire, and made copper sheets and bolts for the bottoms of the navy’s wooden ships, which kept them free of weed and worm: they were copper-bottomed. When the Royal Mint failed to make enough small change, he struck his own copper pennies, with a druid’s head on them, which were spent all over Britain. His miners called him Twm Chwarae Teg, Tom Fair Play."],
   ["Yn 1768 cafwyd gwythïen fawr o fwyn copr ar Fynydd Parys ger Amlwch. Cymerodd Thomas Williams o Lanidan, cyfreithiwr gwlad, achos y tirfeddianwyr, ac erbyn y 1780au ef oedd meistr y gweithfeydd. Daeth Mynydd Parys yn waith copr mwyaf y byd, a thyfodd Amlwch o bentref bach yn un o drefi mwyaf Cymru.",
    "Cododd Williams weithfeydd toddi yn Nhreffynnon, yn Abertawe ac yn Swydd Gaerhirfryn, a gwnaeth haenau a bolltau copr i waelodion llongau pren y Llynges, gan eu cadw’n rhydd rhag chwyn a phryfed: roedd ganddynt waelod copr. Pan fethodd y Bathdy Brenhinol â gwneud digon o arian mân, trawodd ei geiniogau copr ei hun, â phen derwydd arnynt, a gâi eu gwario ledled Prydain. Galwai ei fwynwyr ef yn Twm Chwarae Teg."],
   first=("world", "Ran the greatest copper mine in the world, Parys Mountain, 1780s", "Rheolodd waith copr mwyaf y byd, Mynydd Parys, yn y 1780au", 1780)),
 P("ceiriog", "industrial", "1832–1887", "Ceiriog", "Ceiriog", "The poet of the songs of Wales", "Bardd caneuon Cymru", "John_Ceiriog_Hughes", "words",
   "John Ceiriog Hughes, the most loved lyric poet of nineteenth-century Wales, who wrote the words to many of the songs the Welsh still sing.",
   "John Ceiriog Hughes, telynegwr mwyaf annwyl Cymru’r bedwaredd ganrif ar bymtheg, a ysgrifennodd eiriau llawer o’r caneuon y mae’r Cymry’n eu canu o hyd.",
   ["John Ceiriog Hughes was born in 1832 on the farm of Penybryn, above Llanarmon in the Ceiriog valley, and took his bardic name from the river. He went to work in Manchester as a young man, where the Welsh poets of the city gathered round him, and he won his first fame at the Llangollen eisteddfod of 1858. Later he came home to work on the railways of mid Wales, at Llanidloes and then at Caersws, where he died in 1887.",
    "His short, simple, singing poems went straight to the heart of the nation. He wrote Welsh words for the old airs, among them the song for the Prince of Wales, Ar Dywysog Gwlad y Bryniau, and lyrics such as Nant y Mynydd that every Welsh child knew. His long poem Alun Mabon holds the lines ‘Aros mae’r mynyddau mawr, rhuo trostynt mae y gwynt’, the great mountains remain, the wind roars over them, which gave Gwynfor Evans the title of his history of Wales, Aros Mae."],
   ["Ganed John Ceiriog Hughes yn 1832 ar fferm Penybryn, uwchben Llanarmon yn Nyffryn Ceiriog, a chymerodd ei enw barddol o’r afon. Aeth i weithio i Fanceinion yn ŵr ifanc, lle casglodd beirdd Cymraeg y ddinas o’i gwmpas, ac enillodd ei glod cyntaf yn eisteddfod Llangollen yn 1858. Yn ddiweddarach daeth adref i weithio ar reilffyrdd canolbarth Cymru, yn Llanidloes ac yna yng Nghaersŵs, lle bu farw yn 1887.",
    "Aeth ei gerddi byrion, syml, canadwy yn syth i galon y genedl. Ysgrifennodd eiriau Cymraeg i’r hen alawon, yn eu plith y gân i Dywysog Cymru, Ar Dywysog Gwlad y Bryniau, a thelynegion fel Nant y Mynydd a adwaenai pob plentyn yng Nghymru. Yn ei gerdd hir Alun Mabon mae’r llinellau ‘Aros mae’r mynyddau mawr, rhuo trostynt mae y gwynt’, a roddodd i Gwynfor Evans deitl ei hanes Cymru, Aros Mae."]),
 P("islwyn", "industrial", "1832–1878", "Islwyn", "Islwyn", "The poet of Y Storm", "Bardd Y Storm", "William_Thomas_(Islwyn)", "words",
   "William Thomas of Ynysddu in Gwent, the great thinking poet of Victorian Wales, whose long poem Y Storm sought the meaning of life after the death of the woman he loved.",
   "William Thomas o Ynys-ddu yng Ngwent, bardd meddylgar mawr Cymru oes Victoria, a geisiodd ystyr bywyd yn ei gerdd hir Y Storm wedi marw’r ferch a garai.",
   ["William Thomas was born in 1832 at Ynysddu in the Sirhowy valley, and took his bardic name from the mountain parish of Mynyddislwyn above it. He trained as a surveyor and then for the ministry, and preached all over Wales, though he never took charge of a chapel of his own.",
    "In 1853 Ann Bowen, the young woman he was to marry, died suddenly, and from his grief came Y Storm, The Storm, a long poem in which he wrestled with death, nature, God and eternity. It was the deepest poetry written in Welsh in his century. He is remembered too for the hymn Gwêl uwchlaw cymylau amser, which looks beyond the clouds of time to the land beyond. The borough of Islwyn in Gwent was named after him."],
   ["Ganed William Thomas yn 1832 yn Ynys-ddu yng Nghwm Sirhywi, a chymerodd ei enw barddol o blwyf mynyddig Mynyddislwyn uwchben. Hyfforddodd fel tirfesurydd ac yna ar gyfer y weinidogaeth, a phregethodd ledled Cymru, er na chymerodd ofal capel ei hun erioed.",
    "Yn 1853 bu farw Ann Bowen, y ferch ifanc yr oedd i’w phriodi, yn sydyn, ac o’i alar y daeth Y Storm, cerdd hir lle mae’n ymgodymu ag angau, natur, Duw a thragwyddoldeb. Hon oedd y farddoniaeth ddyfnaf a ysgrifennwyd yn Gymraeg yn ei ganrif. Fe’i cofir hefyd am yr emyn Gwêl uwchlaw cymylau amser, sy’n edrych y tu hwnt i gymylau amser i’r wlad tu draw. Enwyd bwrdeistref Islwyn yng Ngwent ar ei ôl."]),

 # ---------- Modern Wales ----------
 P("j-e-lloyd", "modern", "1861–1947", "Sir John Edward Lloyd", "Syr John Edward Lloyd", "The historian of the princes", "Hanesydd y tywysogion", "John_Edward_Lloyd", "learning",
   "The historian who wrote the first full history of Wales from the earliest times to 1282, and edited the Dictionary of Welsh Biography.",
   "Yr hanesydd a ysgrifennodd yr hanes llawn cyntaf o Gymru o’r cyfnod cynharaf hyd 1282, ac a olygodd y Bywgraffiadur Cymreig.",
   ["John Edward Lloyd was born in Liverpool to Welsh parents in 1861 and studied at Aberystwyth and Oxford. He spent his working life at Bangor, where he was professor of history from 1899 to 1930. In 1911 he published A History of Wales from the Earliest Times to the Edwardian Conquest, two volumes that told for the first time, from the sources, the whole story of the Welsh kingdoms and princes.",
    "It was the book on which every later history of medieval Wales was built, and it gave the Welsh a history to be proud of, set down with the care of a scholar. He went on to write the life of Owain Glyndŵr in 1931, and in his last years he edited Y Bywgraffiadur Cymreig, the Dictionary of Welsh Biography, which tells the lives of thousands of Welsh men and women and is still read today. He was knighted in 1934."],
   ["Ganed John Edward Lloyd yn Lerpwl i rieni Cymreig yn 1861 ac astudiodd yn Aberystwyth a Rhydychen. Treuliodd ei oes waith ym Mangor, lle bu’n athro hanes o 1899 hyd 1930. Yn 1911 cyhoeddodd A History of Wales from the Earliest Times to the Edwardian Conquest, dwy gyfrol a adroddodd am y tro cyntaf, o’r ffynonellau, holl hanes teyrnasoedd a thywysogion Cymru.",
    "Dyma’r llyfr y codwyd pob hanes diweddarach o Gymru’r Oesoedd Canol arno, a rhoddodd i’r Cymry hanes i ymfalchïo ynddo, wedi ei osod i lawr â gofal ysgolhaig. Ysgrifennodd fuchedd Owain Glyndŵr yn 1931, ac yn ei flynyddoedd olaf golygodd Y Bywgraffiadur Cymreig, sy’n adrodd bywydau miloedd o Gymry ac sy’n cael ei ddarllen o hyd. Fe’i hurddwyd yn farchog yn 1934."]),
 P("john-morris-jones", "modern", "1864–1929", "Sir John Morris-Jones", "Syr John Morris-Jones", "Who set the rules of Welsh and of cynghanedd", "A osododd reolau’r Gymraeg a’r gynghanedd", "John_Morris-Jones", "learning",
   "The Bangor professor who wrote the great grammar of Welsh, set down the rules of cynghanedd in Cerdd Dafod, and gave Welsh the spelling it uses today.",
   "Athro Bangor a ysgrifennodd ramadeg mawr y Gymraeg, gosod rheolau’r gynghanedd yn Cerdd Dafod, a rhoi i’r Gymraeg yr orgraff a ddefnyddia heddiw.",
   ["John Morris-Jones was born in 1864 at Trefor in Môn and grew up in Llanfairpwll. At Oxford he and his friend O. M. Edwards founded the Dafydd ap Gwilym Society, and in 1895 he became the first professor of Welsh at Bangor. He set out to give the Welsh language the scholarship it deserved, and to rescue its written form from the confusions of the nineteenth century.",
    "His Welsh Grammar of 1913 traced the history of the language from its roots, and his Cerdd Dafod of 1925 set down the rules of cynghanedd and the strict metres so clearly that poets still learn from it. The committee he led gave Welsh its modern spelling in 1928. He was a fine poet too, and his Welsh version of the Rubáiyát of Omar Khayyám is still loved. He was knighted in 1918."],
   ["Ganed John Morris-Jones yn 1864 yn Nhrefor ym Môn a magwyd ef yn Llanfairpwll. Yn Rhydychen sefydlodd ef a’i gyfaill O. M. Edwards Gymdeithas Dafydd ap Gwilym, ac yn 1895 daeth yn athro Cymraeg cyntaf Bangor. Aeth ati i roi i’r Gymraeg yr ysgolheictod a haeddai, ac i achub ei ffurf ysgrifenedig rhag dryswch y bedwaredd ganrif ar bymtheg.",
    "Olrheiniodd ei Welsh Grammar yn 1913 hanes yr iaith o’i gwreiddiau, a gosododd ei Cerdd Dafod yn 1925 reolau’r gynghanedd a’r mesurau caeth mor eglur nes bod beirdd yn dysgu ohono o hyd. Rhoddodd y pwyllgor a arweiniodd ei horgraff fodern i’r Gymraeg yn 1928. Roedd yn fardd gwych hefyd, ac mae ei fersiwn Gymraeg o Benillion Omar Khayyâm yn annwyl o hyd. Fe’i hurddwyd yn farchog yn 1918."]),
 P("t-gwynn-jones", "modern", "1871–1949", "T. Gwynn Jones", "T. Gwynn Jones", "Poet of the great Welsh revival in verse", "Bardd y deffroad mawr mewn barddoniaeth", "T._Gwynn_Jones", "words",
   "Journalist, scholar and one of the greatest Welsh poets of the twentieth century, whose winning poem of 1902 opened a new age of Welsh poetry.",
   "Newyddiadurwr, ysgolhaig ac un o feirdd Cymraeg mwyaf yr ugeinfed ganrif, a agorodd ei awdl fuddugol yn 1902 oes newydd ym marddoniaeth Gymraeg.",
   ["Thomas Gwynn Jones was born in 1871 at Betws-yn-Rhos, the son of a farmer and poet, and left school young to work as a journalist. He taught himself the old poetry of Wales, and at the Bangor eisteddfod of 1902 he won the chair with Ymadawiad Arthur, a poem of rich and noble strict-metre verse that showed what cynghanedd could do in the new century.",
    "He went on to write the long poems that made his name, among them Madog, Broseliawnd and Anatiomaros, drawing on the old tales of the Britons. From 1919 to 1937 he was professor of Welsh literature at Aberystwyth. He translated Goethe’s Faust and many other works into Welsh, and he gave the language a body of poetry as fine as any in Europe."],
   ["Ganed Thomas Gwynn Jones yn 1871 ym Metws-yn-Rhos, mab i ffermwr a bardd, a gadawodd yr ysgol yn ifanc i weithio fel newyddiadurwr. Dysgodd hen farddoniaeth Cymru iddo’i hun, ac yn eisteddfod Bangor yn 1902 enillodd y gadair ag Ymadawiad Arthur, awdl gyfoethog ac urddasol a ddangosodd yr hyn y gallai’r gynghanedd ei wneud yn y ganrif newydd.",
    "Aeth ymlaen i ysgrifennu’r cerddi hir a wnaeth ei enw, yn eu plith Madog, Broseliawnd ac Anatiomaros, gan dynnu ar hen chwedlau’r Brythoniaid. O 1919 hyd 1937 bu’n athro llenyddiaeth Gymraeg yn Aberystwyth. Cyfieithodd Faust Goethe a llawer o weithiau eraill i’r Gymraeg, a rhoddodd i’r iaith gorff o farddoniaeth cystal ag unrhyw un yn Ewrop."]),
 P("ifor-williams", "modern", "1881–1965", "Sir Ifor Williams", "Syr Ifor Williams", "Who opened the oldest Welsh poetry", "A agorodd yr hen ganu Cymraeg", "Ifor_Williams", "learning",
   "The scholar who showed that the poems of Aneirin and Taliesin really belong to the sixth century, and edited the Four Branches of the Mabinogi.",
   "Yr ysgolhaig a ddangosodd fod cerddi Aneirin a Thaliesin yn perthyn i’r chweched ganrif mewn gwirionedd, ac a olygodd Bedair Cainc y Mabinogi.",
   ["Ifor Williams was born in 1881 at Tregarth, near Bethesda, and spent his whole life at Bangor, where he became professor of Welsh in 1929. He gave his life to the oldest Welsh writing: the poems in the Book of Aneirin and the Book of Taliesin, the englynion of Llywarch Hen and Heledd, and the Four Branches of the Mabinogi, which he edited in 1930.",
    "Word by word he showed that the language of Y Gododdin and of Taliesin’s poems to Urien belongs to the age of the poets themselves, around the year 600. His Canu Aneirin of 1938 and Canu Taliesin of 1960 proved to the world that the Welsh have poetry older than that of almost any other nation in Europe that still speaks its language. He was knighted in 1947."],
   ["Ganed Ifor Williams yn 1881 yn Nhregarth, ger Bethesda, a threuliodd ei oes gyfan ym Mangor, lle daeth yn athro’r Gymraeg yn 1929. Rhoddodd ei fywyd i’r ysgrifennu Cymraeg hynaf: y cerddi yn Llyfr Aneirin a Llyfr Taliesin, englynion Llywarch Hen a Heledd, a Phedair Cainc y Mabinogi, a olygodd yn 1930.",
    "Air wrth air dangosodd fod iaith y Gododdin a cherddi Taliesin i Urien yn perthyn i oes y beirdd eu hunain, tua’r flwyddyn 600. Profodd ei Canu Aneirin yn 1938 a’i Canu Taliesin yn 1960 i’r byd fod gan y Cymry farddoniaeth hŷn na bron unrhyw genedl arall yn Ewrop sy’n siarad ei hiaith o hyd. Fe’i hurddwyd yn farchog yn 1947."]),
 P("r-williams-parry", "modern", "1884–1956", "R. Williams Parry", "R. Williams Parry", "Bardd yr Haf, the poet of summer", "Bardd yr Haf", "R._Williams_Parry", "words",
   "The poet of Dyffryn Nantlle who won the chair in 1910 with Yr Haf and wrote some of the best-loved poems in the Welsh language, among them his englynion to Hedd Wyn.",
   "Bardd Dyffryn Nantlle a enillodd y gadair yn 1910 ag Yr Haf ac a ysgrifennodd rai o gerddi mwyaf annwyl y Gymraeg, yn eu plith ei englynion i Hedd Wyn.",
   ["Robert Williams Parry was born at Tal-y-sarn in the slate country of Dyffryn Nantlle in 1884. At the Colwyn Bay eisteddfod of 1910 he won the chair with Yr Haf, Summer, a poem so rich and lovely that he was known ever after as Bardd yr Haf, the poet of summer. He spent most of his working life teaching at Bangor.",
    "His englynion in memory of Hedd Wyn, the poet killed in 1917 before he could take his chair, are among the most famous in Welsh: ‘Y bardd trwm dan bridd tramor’, the heavy-hearted poet under foreign soil. In 1936 he wrote in support of the three who set fire to the bombing school at Penyberth. His two books, Yr Haf a Cherddi Eraill and Cerddi’r Gaeaf, hold poems of nature, loss and Wales that are still learnt by heart."],
   ["Ganed Robert Williams Parry yn Nhal-y-sarn yng ngwlad y llechi yn Nyffryn Nantlle yn 1884. Yn eisteddfod Bae Colwyn yn 1910 enillodd y gadair ag Yr Haf, awdl mor gyfoethog a hyfryd nes iddo gael ei adnabod byth wedyn fel Bardd yr Haf. Treuliodd y rhan fwyaf o’i oes waith yn dysgu ym Mangor.",
    "Mae ei englynion er cof am Hedd Wyn, y bardd a laddwyd yn 1917 cyn iddo gael eistedd yn ei gadair, ymhlith yr enwocaf yn y Gymraeg: ‘Y bardd trwm dan bridd tramor’. Yn 1936 ysgrifennodd o blaid y tri a roddodd yr ysgol fomio ym Mhenyberth ar dân. Mae ei ddau lyfr, Yr Haf a Cherddi Eraill a Cherddi’r Gaeaf, yn cynnwys cerddi am natur, colled a Chymru sy’n cael eu dysgu ar gof o hyd."]),
 P("gwenallt", "modern", "1899–1968", "Gwenallt", "Gwenallt", "Poet of faith, industry and Wales", "Bardd ffydd, diwydiant a Chymru", "D._Gwenallt_Jones", "words",
   "David James Jones of Pontardawe, twice winner of the chair, who wrote of the tinplate works of his boyhood, the farms of his family in Carmarthenshire, and his faith in God and Wales.",
   "David James Jones o Bontardawe, enillydd y gadair ddwywaith, a ysgrifennodd am weithfeydd alcam ei fachgendod, ffermydd ei deulu yn Sir Gaerfyrddin, a’i ffydd yn Nuw ac yng Nghymru.",
   ["David James Jones was born in 1899 at Pontardawe in the Swansea valley and grew up at Alltwen, where his father worked in the steel and tinplate works. In the First World War he refused to fight and was sent to prison at Wormwood Scrubs and Dartmoor. He won the chair at the National Eisteddfod in 1926 and again in 1931, and taught in the Welsh department at Aberystwyth for almost forty years.",
    "Under the name Gwenallt he wrote poems that joined the two worlds of his life: the works and the chapels of industrial Glamorgan, and the old Welsh-speaking farms of his family at Rhydcymerau in Carmarthenshire, which he mourned as the forestry came in. His poems on Wales, on the Welsh language and on Christ are among the strongest of the century. In 1961 he founded and edited Taliesin, the journal of the Academi Gymreig."],
   ["Ganed David James Jones yn 1899 ym Mhontardawe yng Nghwm Tawe a magwyd ef yn Alltwen, lle gweithiai ei dad yn y gwaith dur ac alcam. Yn y Rhyfel Byd Cyntaf gwrthododd ymladd ac fe’i hanfonwyd i garchar yn Wormwood Scrubs a Dartmoor. Enillodd y gadair yn yr Eisteddfod Genedlaethol yn 1926 ac eto yn 1931, a bu’n dysgu yn adran y Gymraeg yn Aberystwyth am bron i ddeugain mlynedd.",
    "Dan yr enw Gwenallt ysgrifennodd gerddi a unodd ddau fyd ei fywyd: gweithfeydd a chapeli Morgannwg ddiwydiannol, a hen ffermydd Cymraeg ei deulu yn Rhydcymerau yn Sir Gaerfyrddin, y galarodd amdanynt wrth i’r coedwigoedd ddod. Mae ei gerddi ar Gymru, ar yr iaith Gymraeg ac ar Grist ymhlith cryfaf y ganrif. Yn 1961 sefydlodd a golygodd Taliesin, cylchgrawn yr Academi Gymreig."]),
]
